---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import PageBand from '../../components/PageBand.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/Byline.astro';
import PostCard from '../../components/PostCard.astro';
import { cardSummary } from '../../lib/cards.mjs';
import { getSmallest, BEST_YEAR, LISTS_PUBLISHED } from '../../lib/bestof.mjs';
import { imageFor } from '../../lib/core.mjs';
import { listingImage, listingImageAlt, imgFallback, directionsUrl, fmtNum, stars, listJoin, nearText, displayName } from '../../lib/format.mjs';
import { SITE } from '../../config.mjs';
import Photo from '../../components/Photo.astro';
import { pagePhoto, listingPhoto } from '../../lib/photos.mjs';

const post = getSmallest();
const { items } = post;
const photo = pagePhoto(post.url, items.map((i) => i.listing));
const image = photo.src;
const updated = new Date();
const minutes = Math.max(3, Math.round((items.length * 90 + 450) / 230));
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Blog', url: '/blog' }, { name: 'Smallest Waterfalls in the US', url: post.url }];
const states = [...new Set(items.map((i) => i.listing.state))];
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the smallest named waterfall in the US?', a: `Among named waterfalls with a recorded height, <a href="${items[0].listing.url}">${items[0].listing.name}</a> in ${items[0].listing.state} is the smallest at about ${items[0].h.feet} feet${items[1].h.feet === items[0].h.feet ? `, tied with <a href="${items[1].listing.url}">${items[1].listing.name}</a> in ${items[1].listing.state}` : ''}.` },
  { q: 'How were these heights measured?', a: `Each height comes from an explicit figure in the waterfall's USGS Geographic Names Information System record or its Google listing description. We found stated heights for ${post.measured} of the named waterfalls in our directory; most named falls have no official height on record.` },
  { q: 'Why does a 3-foot drop count as a waterfall?', a: 'There is no minimum height in US naming standards. A feature is a waterfall if water drops steeply or vertically over a ledge, and the name is official once it is recorded in the federal gazetteer.' },
  { q: 'Are small waterfalls safer to visit?', a: 'Not always. Low ledges on big rivers can form recirculating currents that trap swimmers. Read <a href="/blog/swimming-at-waterfalls">is it safe to swim at waterfalls</a> before getting in the water.' },
];
const schema = [
  { '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: post.title, description: post.description, image: image.startsWith('http') ? image : `${SITE.url}${image}`, datePublished: LISTS_PUBLISHED.toISOString(), dateModified: updated.toISOString(), mainEntityOfPage: `${SITE.url}${post.url}`, author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'WaterfallsNearYou Editorial Team', url: `${SITE.url}/about` }, publisher: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` } },
  { '@type': 'ItemList', name: post.title, numberOfItems: items.length, itemListElement: items.map((i, n) => ({ '@type': 'ListItem', position: n + 1, url: `${SITE.url}${i.listing.url}`, name: i.listing.name })) },
  { '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '') } })) },
];
---
<Base title={post.title} description={post.description} image={image} imageAlt={photo.alt} type="article" published={LISTS_PUBLISHED.toISOString()} modified={updated.toISOString()} breadcrumbs={crumbs} bandCrumbs schema={schema} preloadImage={image}>
  <PageBand title={post.title} crumbs={crumbs} narrow />
  <article class="wrap narrow">
    <figure class="post-hero">
      <img src={photo.src} alt={photo.alt} width="1200" height="630" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" referrerpolicy="no-referrer" onerror={photo.fallback ? `this.onerror=null;this.src='${photo.fallback}';this.parentNode.querySelector('figcaption')?.remove()` : undefined} />
      {photo.listing && <figcaption>Photo via Google: {photo.listing.name}, {photo.listing.state}, from our directory</figcaption>}
    </figure>
    <Byline published={LISTS_PUBLISHED} updated={updated} minutes={minutes} />
    <nav class="jump" aria-label="Jump to">
      <strong>Jump to:</strong>
      {items.map((i, n) => <>{n ? <span class="sep">·</span> : ''}<a href={`#w${n + 1}`}>{n + 1}. {displayName(i.listing)}</a></>)}
    </nav>
    <div class="prose"><p>Lists of record-breaking waterfalls usually chase height. This one goes the other way: the ten smallest named waterfalls in the United States that have a recorded height, from {items[0].h.feet}-foot ledges to {items[items.length - 1].h.feet}-foot drops across {listJoin(states)}.</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>How We Measured</h2>
        <p>Most named waterfalls have no official height on record. We searched the USGS Geographic Names Information System and Google listing descriptions for every waterfall in our directory and found an explicit height for {post.measured} of them. These ten are the smallest of that group, excluding falls recorded on private land. Heights are approximate and describe the drop, not the width.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Waterfall</th><th>State</th><th>Height</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{items.map((i, n) => <tr><td>{n + 1}</td><td><a href={i.listing.url}>{displayName(i.listing)}</a></td><td><a href={`/states/${i.listing.stateSlug}`}>{i.listing.state}</a></td><td>About {i.h.feet} ft</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ol class="pc-list" style="margin:22px 0 10px">
        {items.map(({ listing: l, h }, n) => (
          <PostCard listing={l} rank={n + 1} anchor={`w${n + 1}`}
            lead={`About ${h.feet} feet tall. ${l.name} is a waterfall ${nearText(l)}, ${l.state}.`}
            why={[`The ${h.source} records it as: "${h.text.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim()}"`, ...(l.os?.rating ? [`Visitors rate it ${l.os.rating.toFixed(1)} stars from ${fmtNum(l.os.reviews)} Google reviews.`] : []), ...(l.nearby?.[0] ? [`The nearest other named waterfall is ${l.nearby[0].listing.name}, about ${l.nearby[0].miles.toFixed(1)} miles to the ${l.nearby[0].direction}.`] : [])]} />
        ))}
      </ol>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why Small Waterfalls Are Worth a Visit</h2>
        <p>Short drops are often the easiest falls to reach, frequently sit in town parks or beside roads, and keep flowing in seasons when tall, thin falls dry up. They are also where geology is easiest to read: a single resistant ledge of rock holding back a stream. For the opposite end of the scale, see the <a href="/blog/largest-waterfalls-in-the-us-by-state">largest waterfalls in the US by state</a>, or learn the difference between a cascade and a plunge in our guide to <a href="/blog/types-of-waterfalls">types of waterfalls</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin:36px 0"><Faq faqs={faqs} heading="Frequently asked questions" /></div>
  </article>
</Base>

